import sys; sys.path.insert(0,'/home/claude/work'); from gen import *
W='/tmp/tlv-src/apps/web'; A='https://apocalypse12.com'; T='https://thefirstbeast.com'
make(13,f"This is a summary. The Fathers on chapter 13 are set out in full on the sister sites: [The Dragon on the Shore]({A}/the-dragon-on-the-shore) and [Two Marks on the Forehead]({A}/two-marks-on-the-forehead) on The Woman and the Ark, and mark by mark on [The First Beast]({T}/marks). None of them names a living person, and neither does this site.",
[('/chapter-13/the-first-beast','The First Beast'),('/chapter-13/the-second-beast','The Second Beast'),('/chapter-13/mark-of-the-beast','The Mark of the Beast'),('/chapter-13/666-number-of-the-beast','666'),(f'{T}/','The First Beast: Revelation 13 in depth')],
[('13:1–4','The beast from the sea',[
 ('What rises from the sea.','St. Andrew of Caesarea, following St. Methodius and St. Hippolytus: the Antichrist, coming up out of the troubled sea of human life. Oecumenius: a chief of the demons under Satan. Tyconius: “a people arising from a people,” the body of the devil; in this book the beast is sometimes the devil, sometimes his body, sometimes its leaders, and the reader must judge passage by passage.'),
 ('“The dragon gave it his own power.”','Oecumenius: the power of the apostate dragon dwells in these deceits, for he is their instigator and teacher.'),
 ('The head that seemed slain.','Tyconius sees a counterfeit of the Lord’s Resurrection, offered so that the beast may be received in place of Christ.'),
]),
('13:5–10','Forty-two months, and the endurance of the saints',[
 ('A limited time.','Oecumenius: beside the unending ages, all time is short. The proud mouth is Isaiah’s “I will ascend to the heavens” (Isaiah 14:13–14).'),
 ('Not everyone.','Oecumenius notes how carefully the vision limits “all who dwell on earth”: there were always some who kept themselves from the devil’s worship.'),
]),
('13:11–15','The beast from the earth',[
 ('A lamb’s horns, a dragon’s voice.','Oecumenius notices the wording: horns “like” a lamb’s, speech “like” a dragon’s. He pretends to be the Christ and is not. St. Victorinus: he has the appearance of a just man and is full of the devil’s malice. Tyconius: the two horns are the two Testaments, which belong to the true Lamb and are here usurped.'),
 ('Fire from heaven.','Primasius sees a counterfeit of Pentecost. Only the Church of Christ has that gift.'),
]),
('13:16–18','The mark and the number',[
 ('The mark.','The Fathers treat it as a counterfeit, joined to worship. St. Hippolytus gives the oldest explanation: no one will buy or sell without first offering incense. Primasius and St. Caesarius of Arles read the hand as works and the forehead as the confession of faith. Andrew: those whose faces are sealed with the divine light will not receive it.'),
 ('The number of the name.','St. Irenaeus, about 180, is the earliest: the number gathers up all apostasy. He offers possible names and refuses to settle on one, since the Spirit would have said the name plainly had it been needed. Oecumenius lists more and leaves the choice open. Primasius reads the number of character: words meaning “contrary to honour” and “I deny.”'),
]),
],f'{W}/src/data/fathers/chapter-13.tsx'); wire(13,W)
make(14,f"This is a summary. The Fathers on chapter 14 are set out in full on The Woman and the Ark: [The Lamb on Mount Zion]({A}/the-lamb-on-mount-zion), [Three Angels]({A}/three-angels) and [The Harvest and the Winepress]({A}/the-harvest-and-the-winepress).",
[('/topics/the-144000','The 144,000'),('/topics/babylon','Babylon'),(f'{A}/the-lamb-on-mount-zion','The Lamb on Mount Zion (The Woman and the Ark)')],
[('14:1–5','The Lamb on Mount Zion',[
 ('Who stands with the Lamb?','St. Victorinus and Oecumenius think of Israel coming to faith at the end (Romans 11:25–26). St. Andrew of Caesarea: the mountain is “not the ancient city, but the new city of the living God.” St. Bede reads the Church, and marks the contrast: the beast stands on the sand of the sea, the Lamb on Mount Zion.'),
 ('Who are the “virgins”?','St. Methodius, St. Cyprian, St. Jerome and St. Augustine read consecrated virgins. St. Caesarius of Arles reads the whole Church that holds to the pure faith (2 Corinthians 11:2). Augustine will not let anyone conclude that the Lamb’s company is closed: the Lord’s garden holds “the lilies of virgins, and the ivy of married people and the violets of widows.”'),
]),
('14:6–7','The eternal gospel',[
 ('The angel in mid-heaven.','Victorinus: Elijah. Primasius: the Church’s preachers, one messenger because the Church is one. Oecumenius: an angel of high rank.'),
 ('Whom to fear.','Andrew: fear God, and not the Antichrist, whose power lasts only a short time and cannot kill the soul.'),
]),
('14:8–13','Babylon fallen; the blessed dead',[
 ('What “Babylon” means.','Tyconius: the city and people of the devil, one city made of all the nations that error has made drunk. Oecumenius: the confusion of this present life. Andrew widens the wine to all sin.'),
 ('Why “fallen,” when she still stands?','Tyconius: Scripture often speaks of what is to come as already past, because what it foretells is certain.'),
]),
('14:14–20','The harvest and the winepress',[
 ('The one on the white cloud','is Christ, for all the Fathers cited. The cloud is his mother, on whom the Lord rode when he took flesh (Oecumenius); or his own flesh (Primasius, Bede); or his body the Church, white after persecution (Caesarius).'),
 ('One harvest or two?','Oecumenius: two, the grain for the just and sinners together, the vintage for the gravest transgressors. Caesarius and Bede: one, told twice, since the Church is both the Lord’s field and his vineyard.'),
 ('The blood and the sixteen hundred stadia.','The Fathers do not take these as measurements.'),
]),
],f'{W}/src/data/fathers/chapter-14.tsx'); wire(14,W)
